<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kapuas River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kapuas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kapuas is the longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, flowing about 1,140 kilometres west across West Kalimantan on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a>. Pontianak stands near its mouth, and the river has long been the main route into the interior, carrying gold, forest products and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a>. Malay sultanates, Chinese mining communities and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dayak-people">Dayak peoples</a> lived along its course.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kapuas-river.jpg" alt="Kapuas River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Achmad Rabin Taim from Jakarta, Indonesia · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_Kapuas_River.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
